So this is purely a design task. I should now craft a plan-stage response addressing the three points requested by the researcher:
(1) Measuring method — end-to-end 4-bit quantization, prefill vs decoding metrics (stage latency, generation throughput, sequence length & batch effects, memory), and how to measure each stage separately.
(2) Comparison/baselines — fair control groups (FP16/BF16 baseline, optional alternative quantization schemes) to attribute prefill/decode differences to QuaRot.
(3) Key assumptions and uncertainties — model scale (7B/13B/70B), hardware, quantization details (weight/activation/KV-cache), batch size and sequence length.

I need to give a coherent outline (not full design), based on knowledge of QuaRot. Let me recall what I know about QuaRot:

QuaRot (arXiv 2404.00456, "QuaRot: Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs") — introduces rotation-based outlier elimination so that 4-bit quantization can be applied to weights, activations and KV cache (all end-to-end 4-bit) in LLMs like LLaMa-2. It uses random Hadamard rotations and online/offline rotation to remove outliers from activations/weights, enabling full end-to-end quantization. They report improvements in memory footprint, latency (decode speedup) and perplexity. They report e.g. for LLaMa-2-70B end-to-end 4-bit, ~3x memory saving, and decode throughput improvements.

Key points about QuaRot's approach:
- Preprocessing: rotate weight matrices offline, activate (Hadamard) rotation fused into preceding RMSNorm / embedding layer.
- End-to-end: weights, activations, KV cache all 4-bit (RTN / group-wise quantization, e.g., 128 group size).
- They evaluate perplexity on WikiText-2, and throughput on an A100 (decode throughput measured via transformers with their custom implementation, using batch size 1 and large batch).

The question is about prefill and decoding performance of LLaMa-2 models.

Let me think about the design outline.

Measurement approach:
- Prefill phase: the model processes the input prompt in parallel. Metrics: prefill latency per token (average time to process the prompt), prefill compute throughput (tokens/sec), possibly using attention mask with causal masking, KV cache populated. Measure with controlled prompt length L (e.g., 512, 2048) and batch size B.
- Decoding phase: autoregressive token-by-token generation. Metrics: decoding throughput (tokens/s per batch / aggregate), latency per generated token (inter-token latency / TTFT+ after prefill), time-to-first-token (TTFT) is partly prefill. Also generation throughput at batch B. Also end-to-end total latency for fixed output length.
